messages.text:
The article with our result images and short clips looks allowed; the website copy needs Fastwork's written OK first.
- This is research from the law texts, not legal advice. Thai Copyright Act sections 32-33 allow non-profit study and review with credit, and quoting "in part" with credit.
- Code (section 35): study or review is allowed only if not for profit. A whole working site copy goes beyond quoting "in part", so ask first, as you planned.
- Fastwork's terms (buyer and seller): no clause found on copying, scraping or reverse engineering. The operator is named as Changesy Co., Ltd., so confirm whom to address.
- Vercel: its built-in password lock needs the Pro plan ($20/month per project). The free (Hobby) plan works with a small middleware check of one password hash.
- Free-plan rule: personal, non-commercial use only. An unpaid study demo fits.
- Demo must-haves:
  - cover every path, including scripts and images;
  - add a "noindex" header so search engines skip it;
  - strip the analytics scripts (Amplitude, Hotjar, Google Tag Manager);
  - drop or ask about Fastwork's font and logo.
- Email contents (Columbia University guidance):
  - who you are, and exactly what you'd use;
  - non-commercial and educational, with credit and "not affiliated";
  - password-only demo, taken down on request;
  - ask for a written reply saying what is allowed.
- Not settled: whether a password-only link sent to one company counts as "public" under Thai law.
What you need to do: tell me "draft the email" if you want it. You send it yourself; we publish nothing until Fastwork says yes in writing.

The private site is built and tested on this Mac; nothing is online.
- Tracking removed: Google Tag Manager (which loads Analytics, Amplitude and Hotjar) and Cloudflare's beacon. A scan finds no tracking left.
- Lock: the browser's own password box on every file; any user name works, only the password counts. No password: 67 of 67 lock tests are refused.
- Look: same layout and height as v10 at 6 scroll points; no errors from the lock or from removed scripts.
- Unproven until the first upload: one Vercel detail (passing the request on to the file). The guide has the check and a fallback.
- My defaults, unless you say otherwise:
  - built from the copy we measured;
  - the waitlist form stays dead (it sends nothing);
  - the browser's password box instead of a login page;
  - a project name without "fastwork";
  - the password goes to Fastwork in a separate message;
  - the site is deleted after their reply.
- The guide (research/private-demo/README.md) lists your steps: pick a password, run hash-password.mjs, create the Vercel project, set 2 settings, upload, check.
What you need to do: before any upload, type "yes, prepare the public source copy" here. Then I'll post the exact steps for you to run.

The next step is your one-line OK: type "yes, prepare the public source copy". Then I post the exact commands, and you run them from your own Vercel account.
- Why the OK first: our standing rule says nothing from this project leaves the Mac without it. The upload happens under your name.
- Then you run 8 steps (about 15 min):
  - pick a strong password and run the hash script, which prints 2 values;
  - create a Vercel project from the demo folder only (never from git);
  - add the 2 values as Sensitive settings and turn off Vercel's own login wall;
  - deploy, then check that the page asks for the password and shows v10.
- Before the upload I copy the demo folder outside this project, so the upload can't include anything else.
- After the upload: the link and the password go to Fastwork in separate messages, and you delete the project when they reply.
What you need to do: type "yes, prepare the public source copy" here when you're ready.
